    "name": "Preamble — Parties and Purpose",
    "text": "This letter sets forth the agreement of Plaintiff Lincoln General Insurance Qpmp..any (\"Plaintiff= or \"Lincoln General\") and U.S. Auto Insurance Services, Inc. (°U.S. Auto’), G~rnma Group Inc., CSI Agency Services, Inc., Alpha Partners, Ltd., James Douglas Maxwell’ a/Ida Doug Maxwell, James Thornton Maxwell a/ida Jim Maxwoll, and Santa Fe Auto Insurance Company (these original and proposed additiona! defendant~to the above suit are referred to herein as the \"Defendants-) (collectively Plaintiff and Defendants are the \"Parties\") regarding the scope of the. above referenced lawsuit (the \"Lawsuit’) and the tolling of certain claims."
  },
  {
    "id": "c002",
    "name": "Limitation of Scope of Lincoln General's Claims in the Lawsuit",
    "text": "The Parties are presently engaged in the Lawsuit which relates t~ a reinsurance arrangement through which Lincoln General agreed to act as reinsurer for certain private passenger automobile insurance p01icles sold and handled by U.S. Auto and, allegedly, other Defendants, and for which State and County Mutual Fire Insuran~ce Company (\"State and County\") was the ceding insurer. Lincoln General hereby agrees to limit the scope of its claims in the Lawsuit to the following: .. Claims related to alleged inflation or miscalculation of commissions, return commissions, or commission adjustments for U.S. Auto pursuant to the reinsurance arrangement as a result of alleged exclusiqn or misapplication of Incurred But Not Reported losses (\"IBNR-) in calculating such commissions, return commlssions, or commission adjustments. This includes, but is not limited to, claims related to alleged failure to .timely remit amounts allegedly owing to Lincoln General as a consequence of alleged inflation or miscalculation of Auto’s commissions, return commissions, or commission adjustments; claims related to alleged improper retention, misuse, or conversion by Defendants andlor other persons or entities of any such alleged amounts owing to but not timely paid to Lincoln General, including ~nds that Lincoln General asserts were =to be held in trust; and claims related to alleged encouragement or involvement by any Defendant or other person or entity in any of the foregoing alleged conduct. Accordingly, such claims may encompass actions seeking legal or equitable relief for, among other things, breach of contract, breach of fiduciary duty and/or trust, aiding and abetting breach of fiduciary duty andlor trust, tortious interference with contract, misappropriation, conversion, unjust Atlanta, Ausl|n, Boston, Chicago, Dallas, Houston, London, Los Angeles, New Orleans. New York, Sacramento, Washington DC 72 J. ,~moid April 30, 2008 enrichment, and an accounting. (2) Claims related to the alleged practice, by one or more of the Defendants, of paying for insurance claims or losses on insurance policies not reinsured by Lincoln General and/or allegedly making excess payments for insurance claims or losses on insurance policies only partially reinsured by Lincoln General, using an account funded by Lincoln General, without providing Lincoln General with reimbursement for such improper and/or excess payments,"
  },
  {
    "id": "c003",
    "name": "Unnumbered paragraph — \"Other Causes of Action\" reservation (April 30, 2008 letter)",
    "text": "In addition, Lincoln General. agrees not to asseit as part of the Lawsuit any causes of action for alleged mishandling of insurance claims on the reinsured policies, alleged setting of improper reserves for the payment of losses on the reinsured policies (other than concerning the exclusion or misapplication of IBNR), alleged improper closing of claims files for insurance claims on the reinsured policies, or U.S. Auto’s decision to stop writing new policies to’ be reinsured by Lincoln General beginning on or about May 2007 (collectively, \"Other Causes of Action\"); however, Lincoln General reserves the right to assert one or more of the Other Causes of Action as part of a separate lawsuit. Any separate lawsuit involving Other Causes 6f A6tion, howe.vet, may n.pt be. filed by Lincoln General during the pendency of the Lawsuit"
  },
  {
    "id": "c004",
    "name": "Excess Funds Claim(s) — Separate Lawsuit, Waiver and Tolling",
    "text": "Lincoln General further agre~ to enable Defendarti U.S. Auto :lnsuranc~ Services, Inc, (\"U,S. Auto\") to limit the claims it might otherwise seek in the Lawsuit. In particular, Lincoln General agrees that U.So Auto may, in a separate lawsuit, assert claims (including, but not limited to a breach of contract claim), based on an allegation that Lincoln General has withheld or retained monies that exceed the maximum profit margin to which U.S. Auto contends Lincoln General is entitled under the 2002 and/or 2003 Quota Share Reinsurance Agreements (\"the E.xcess Funds Claim(s).\" Lincoln General agrees that the Excess Funds Claim(s) do not adse out of the same nucleus of operative facts as any claims or counterclaims asserted in the Lawsuit, and thus may appropriately be brought in a separate lawsuit. Further, Lincoln General expressly relinquishes and waives its dght to assert that the Excess Funds Claim(s) are barred by the doctrines of ms judicata or collateral estoppel as a result of any findings of fact, conclusions of law, judgments, verdicts, arbitration decisions, o.r other determinations made as part of the Lawsuit. Lincoln General also agrees to toll the running of the statute of limitations for the the Excess Funds Claim(s) until the Lawsuit has been settled or otherwise finally resolved, including the completion of any appeals. By agreeing to toll the running of the statute of limitations in this fashion, Lincoln General is not waiving its right or ability to assert any limitations defenses which may exist immediately before this agreement is executed. Finally, any separate lawsuit involving the Excess Funds Claim(s) may not be filed by U.S. Auto dudng the penden.cy of the Lawsuit."
  },
  {
    "id": "c005",
    "name": "Defendants' Reciprocal Agreement — Other Causes of Action, Waiver and Tolling",
    "text": "In excl~ange, Defendants agree that the Other Causes of Action do not arise out of the same nucleus of operative facts as any claims or c~untercla!ms asserted in the Lawsuit and thus may appropriately be brought in a separate lawsuit. Further, Defendants expressly relinquish and waive their right to assert that the Other Causes of Action are barred by the doctrines of ms judicata or collateral esloppel as a result of any fir~dings of fact, conclusions of la..w, judgments, verdicts, arbitration decisions, or other determinations made as part of the Lawsuit, In addition, Defendants agree to toll the running of the statute of limitations for the Other Causes of Action i~nt~ the .Lawsuit has been settled or otherwise finally resolved, including the completion of any appeals. By agreeing to toll the running of the statute of limitations in this fashion, Defendants are not waiving their dght or ab~ty to assert any limitations defenses which 73 J. Arnold April 30, 2008 may exist immediately before this agreement is executed. Moreover, by entering into this agreement, in general, Defendants do not concede or acknowledge they are proper parties to ¯ the Lawsuit, or to any separate lawsuit that may eventually be filed."
  },
  {
    "id": "c006",
    "name": "No Acknowledgment of Merit; Reservation of Defenses",
    "text": "Lastly, it is understood by Uncoln General-that Defendants, by executing this agreement, are not acknowledging that the Other Causes of Action have any medt whatsoever. Likewise, it is understood by Defendants that Lincoln General, by executing this agreement, is not acknowledging that the Excess Fund Claim(s) have any medt whatsoever. Lincoln General and Defer~dants, In answer or other response to any separate lawsuit that is or may be eventually filed, all reserve the dght to contest any later-filed separate lawsuits, and may assert any and all applicable defenses or affirmative defenses to allegations, claims, or counterclaims that are alleged in such lawsuits."
  }
